# MDX Content Setup

## Frontmatter Setup

All content pages must include a YAML frontmatter block with at least a title and description. Optional fields like date, tags, or draft status can be added manually as needed.

Example frontmatter structure:
```yaml
---
title: "Page Title"
description: "Brief summary."
date: 2025-01-01
tags: [docs, api]
draft: false
---
```

Validation is handled with Yup schemas (alternative to Zod). Example:
```typescript
import * as yup from 'yup';

const contentSchema = yup.object({
  title: yup.string().required(),
  description: yup.string().required(),
  date: yup.date().optional(),
  tags: yup.array(yup.string()).optional(),
  draft: yup.boolean().default(false),
});
```

## MDX Plugin Configuration

Add the following plugins in your Astro config to process Markdown and MDX files:

```javascript
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import mdx from '@astrojs/mdx';
import remarkGfm from 'remark-gfm';
import rehypeSlug from 'rehype-slug';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [mdx()],
  markdown: {
    remarkPlugins: [remarkGfm],
    rehypePlugins: [rehypeSlug],
  },
});
```

For syntax highlighting, use the built-in Prism integration (not Shiki):
```javascript
export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    syntaxHighlight: 'prism',
  },
});
```

## Content Organization

Place your content files in a single flat folder under `src/content/`. For example:
```
src/content/
  getting-started.md
  api-reference.md
  changelog.md
```

If you need separate sections, use subfolders but treat them as plain directories — collections are not used. Astro will generate routes based on the file name.

## Custom Components in MDX

You can use any Astro or React component inside MDX by importing them in the frontmatter. For a Callout component, create `src/components/Callout.astro` and then import it in each MDX file:

```mdx
---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Callout type="info">
  Important note
</Callout>
```

## Content Queries

To list content, use `Astro.glob()` to fetch all MDX files:

```typescript
const allDocs = await Astro.glob('../content/*.mdx');
```

Sort by a frontmatter `order` field if present; otherwise sort by title:

```typescript
allDocs.sort((a, b) => {
  const orderA = a.frontmatter.order || 999;
  const orderB = b.frontmatter.order || 999;
  if (orderA !== orderB) return orderA - orderB;
  return a.frontmatter.title.localeCompare(b.frontmatter.title);
});
```

## Auto-linking Headings

To add clickable anchor links to headings, wrap them with a custom heading component in the MDX provider. Example using a global `h2` override:

```typescript
import { createElement } from 'react';

export function useMDXComponents() {
  return {
    h2: (props) => <h2 id={props.id}><a href={`#${props.id}`}>{props.children}</a></h2>,
  };
}
```

## Guidelines

- Title and description are required for all pages.
- Use the `draft: true` flag to hide pages in production; filter them in your glob query.
- For multiple content types (docs, guides, changelog), consider using naming conventions (e.g., `doc-getting-started.md`, `guide-setup.md`) instead of separate collections.
- Keep custom components minimal to avoid complexity.

